% c075 (MOLA PDF 5: harp pedalling is left to the performer and not printed in the part): the diagram is score-only (tag 'score)
% c174 (orchestration; Casanelles ch. 6, PDF 286-287: sounds in the same frequency range mask each other; PDF 275-276: the attack transient is the plucked note's short inharmonic part; Adler ch. 4, PDF 113: the harp's lower two octaves are 'somber, dark'): 123 harp p (was pp): its G2-A3 broken chords share the octave with the cellos' bass, Bn 1's counter-line (125-130) and the basses' G2,
% so at pp only the attacks came through; at p, the cellos' level, it is the G-major passage's audible colour. Notes, register and pedals unchanged (c116).
devHarpL = { R1*8 | R1*8 | R1*4 | \tag #'score { R1*4 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "bsnOne" #UP { R1*4^\markup \tiny "Bn. 1" } } | \stemUp g,8\p-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \harp-pedal #"---|-v--" b,8 d8 g8 g,8 b,8 d8 g8 | fis,8 a,8 c8 d8 g,8 b,8 d8 g8 | g,8 b,8 e8 g8 g,8 b,8 e8 g8 | g,8 c8 e8 g8 g,8 b,8 d8 g8 | a,8 c8 e8 a8 a,8 c8 e8 a8 | a,8 d8 fis8 a8 a,8 d8 fis8 a8 | g,8\< b,8 d8 g8 g,8 b,8 d8 g8 | a,8 c8 d8 fis8 a,8 c8 d8 fis8 | \stemNeutral r1\!^\markup \italic "étouffez" R1*3 | }  % c206 (Adler PDF 117, sons étouffés; as at 82 and 243): damp the low D7 arpeggio as the G-major cadence arrives at 131
% c059 (holistic, Tchaikovsky 4/iv; Adler part 1 PDF 152 items 2 and 6, PDF 154, PDF 267): 110-113 the violins take the climax melody in octaves, Vln I G6-A flat6-F6-E6-F6 with Fl 1,
% Vln II G5-A flat5-F5-E5-F5 with Fl 2 and Ob 1 (Vln II's former C5-D flat5 line stays in Ob 2, Cl 1 and Tpt 2). Pitches, harmony and dynamics unchanged.
% c062 (harmony; A&S Unit 29, PDF 503, 505, 510-511): 109 the lament bass's D flat carries a German sixth D flat-F-A flat-B natural (Vln I B flat-B-C rises against the falling bass, Vla A flat), which opens to the octave C and the cadential 6/4 (Vla A flat held, Vln II F-E) before the climactic V7 at 110.
% c063 (form; Adler II PDF 257-258: dialogue, a phrase answered at once in another colour "aids in a clearer exposition of the form"; PDF 274, Beethoven VC iii: the
% bassoon's line set against a contrasting activity, the answer kept two octaves above the woodwind solo): 117 and 122 Vln I, arco p against Vln II's pizz., answers the bassoon's
% held notes with the development's scale figure (winds at 101, 105), so the call of 99-106 carries into the lyrical centre; 117 descends F6-A flat5 to G5 at 118 (sixths and fourths over the solo's G4-F4-E flat4, no fifths); 122 descends D6-F sharp5 with B natural and lands on
% G5 at 123 with the turn to G major. Vln I then rests until its hand-over entry at 128.
% c085 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 37-38: a slur is one bow; an accented note after a run takes a new bow): 103-104 the violins' run closes its slur on E flat, and the accented F is a separate stroke, as the scale figure is bowed at 3-4, 87-90 and 263-264.
% c171 (melody; A&S Unit 3, PDF 50 sec. 3: high notes and dissonant notes attract accent; PDF 52 sec. 7: rhythmic groups; PDF 54 sec. 9: the appoggiatura is an accented dissonance): c131 restored the solo's A4-G4-F4 at 117 (the appoggiatura of F2's third bar), so Vln I's first answer, written by c063 for the old held G4, ran F6-A5 in eighths over the solo's peak and its resolution. The answer moves to 119 beats 2-4, over the solo's held C4 (the consequent's first note, as 122 answers the held A3): G6-F6-E flat 6-D6-C6-A5 down the iio7 (A-C-E flat-G), landing on the leading tone F sharp 5 at 120 (V), as the 122 answer ends G5-F sharp 5 before its G (a B flat 5-A5 ending made fifths with the solo's E flat 4-D4). Notes of the solo unchanged.
% c174 (orchestration; Casanelles ch. 6, PDF 286: a section sounds fuller and brighter than one player; PDF 287: an instrument alone in its frequency range is clear and present; PDF 271-273: the printed level is the level wanted in the result): 119 and 122 Vln I's answers pp (were p, the solo's own level):
% a whole section in G6-F sharp 5, two octaves above the single bassoon and above everything but Vln II's pizz. G5, carries at pp and answers the solo without outweighing it.
% c209 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 58-59 and the note to Ex. 24, PDF 200: a melody in the violins' very high register is isolated unless doubled an octave below; one or two desks keep the upper octave and the rest of Vln I play the octave below, so the piercing top is softened and the line gains a clearer sound; Ex. 26, PDF 202-203, and Ex. 36, PDF 214: two solo violins on top, the rest of Vln I an octave lower, the solo desk prominent enough because the dynamic is pp):
% 119 and 122 Vln I's pp answers to the bassoon solo (G6-A5 | F sharp5; D6-F sharp5 | G5) were the whole section alone at the top of the E string, three octaves above the solo. Now Vln I divide: 2 soli keep the line at pitch (upper note) and the rest double it an octave lower (octave chords, as c109 wrote 159-164) (G5-A4 | F sharp4; D5-F sharp4 | G4). Top line, bassoon solo and all other parts unchanged.
devVlnOne = { R1*4 | r2 r4 f'''8(\mf es'''8 | des'''8 c''' bes'' a'' bes'' c''' des''' es''') | f'''4-> f''4-> r2 | R1 | c'''1~\p\< | c'''2 c'''4--( bes''4) | b''2 c'''4( g''4) | g'''1\ff~ | g'''4( aes'''2 f'''4) | f'''2~( f'''4 e'''4) | f'''2-> r2 | fis''1\p\> | R1*2\! | R1*2 | r4 <g'' g'''>8(\pp^\markup \italic "dolce, div.: 2 soli upper note" <f'' f'''> <es'' es'''> <d'' d'''> <c'' c'''> <a' a''> | <fis' fis''>4) r4 r2 | R1 | r4 <d'' d'''>8(\pp^\markup \italic "div." <c'' c'''> <b' b''> <a' a''> <g' g''> <fis' fis''> | <g' g''>4) r4 r2 | R1*4 | r2 r4 a''4(\mf\<_\markup \italic "unis." | b''2 g''2) | a''1 | r4 a''8(\f b'' c''' d''' e''' fis''' | g'''4->) d'''4-> b''4-> g''4-> | g''1\>~ | g''2. r4\! | }
% c027 (texture; Adler II PDF 264, 267, 274): 115-122 bassoon solo: clarinets tacet (no same-family sustained notes in the solo's register); Vln II pizz. an octave higher (C5-G5; 119 C-E flat, so the top does not move A-D in octaves with the bass into 120), above the solo.
% c063: 119 beat 4 Vln II A4-C5 (was C5-E flat5): its E flat5 moved in octaves with the bassoon solo's E flat4-D4 into 120 (E flat5-D5 on beat 2), heard once the violas' pizz. left the solo's register.
% c067 (texture; Sebesky ch. 3, PDF 140 rule 6 and PDF 142 Ex. 202: the lead line reinforced an octave lower at the climax of a build): 128-130 Vln II leaves Hn 1's G4/F sharp4 pad and plays the tune an octave below Vln I (A4 | B4 G4 | A4), the last step of the scoring crescendo into 131.
% c092 (A&S PDF 76: the 3rd of a triad is never omitted except for special effects; PDF 89 point 4): 134 Vln II B4 (was D5 held), so the G7 keeps its 3rd after Hn 3-4 leave at 133 (G-B-F-G, the 5th omitted as root-position sevenths allow).
% c093 (A&S Unit 14, PDF 214, 228): 116 Vln II pizz. D5-A5 (was D5-F sharp5 over the bass F sharp: the leading tone doubled and both rose to G a beat apart).
% c132: 101 and 105 Cl 1's mf is now the printed one (Cl 2 enters with it on beat 2); Cl 2 carries a hidden mf on its beat-1 rest for the MIDI and prints its own mf in its part.
% c132 (harmony; A&S Unit 23, PDF 394-395, cross relation; PDF 376-377, a dissonance does not sound against its own resolution): 117 the violas rest on beat 2 (their G4-B flat4 pizz. lay around the restored solo A4, with its resolution G4 at the same pitch; Vln II's E flat5-G5 carries the beat); 117 Vln I's run ends B flat5-A5 (was A flat5, a cross relation with the solo's A natural 4 in the same bar); 119 beat 4 Vln II G5 alone (c112's E flat5 there moved in octaves with the solo's E flat4-D4 into 120, the doubling c063 had removed). 101 and 105 Hn 1-2 and Cl 2 enter on beat 2 with Bn 1 (on beat 1 their E-G and A-C sounded against the strings' sf F and B flat chords, a major 7th with the bass that did not resolve; PDF 377 box).
% c152 (harmony; A&S Unit 29, PDF 504 Ex. 29-3: the augmented sixth is the Phrygian cadence's flat 6 with sharp 4 added; PDF 505: the Italian sixth, third doubled, suits a thin texture and goes straight to V; PDF 510: in minor it follows I directly):
%   121 beats 3-4 the bass (Vc, Cb pizz.) takes E flat under the solo's G3 and Vln II's C sharp 5 / G5 with the violas' G4 make an Italian sixth E flat-G-C sharp, which opens to the octave D at 122 (C sharp-D, E flat-D, G-F sharp); 122 violas A4-D5 (was A4-C5: C5 a beat after Vln II's C sharp 5 in the same octave was a cross relation), so the Italian sixth opens to the plain V, as A&S Ex. 29-2 and 29-7 show; the G-minor half cadence now has the flat 6 that the exposition's Phrygian half cadences have at 57-58 and 177-178.
devVlnTwo = { R1*4 | r2 r4 f''8(\mf es''8 | des''8 c'' bes' a' bes' c'' des'' es'') | f''4-> f'4-> r2 | R1 | aes''2\p\< g''2~ | g''2~ g''4 f''4~ | f''2.( e''4) | g''1\ff~ | g''4( aes''2 f''4) | f''2~( f''4 e''4) | f''2-> r2 | c''2.\p\> r4\! | r4 <bes' g''>4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 <d'' g''>4 | r4 <d'' a''>4 r4 <d'' g''>4 | r4 es''4 r4 <es'' g''>4 | r4 <es'' g''>4 r4 <bes' g''>4 | r4 <es'' g''>4 r4 g''4 | r4 <d'' fis''>4 r4 <d'' fis''>4 | r4 <d'' g''>4 r4 <cis'' g''>4 | r4 <d'' fis''>4 r2 | R1*4 | g'1\pp^\markup \italic "arco" | fis'2. a'4(\mf\< | b'2 g'2) | a'1 | r4 fis'8(\f g' a' b' c'' d'' | d''4->) b'4-> g'4-> d'4-> | d''1\> | b'2. r4\! | }
% c041 (melody; Adler ch. 7, PDF 210: the bassoon solo 117 keeps G4 below its weak upper fifth): 125 the violas, who have no such limit, restore F2's third bar with its appoggiatura (A4-G4-F sharp4, was G4 dotted half-F sharp4), so the G-major hand-over states the tune as at 53 and 69.
% c063 (Adler ch. 17, PDF 274: keep the accompanying forces registrally distant from a woodwind soloist; PDF 287, Ex. XVII-15): 115-122 the violas' off-beat pizzicato moves up an octave (G4-D5, between the solo and Vln II's D5-G5);
% at G3-D4 it struck the solo's own pitches (B flat3 115, C4 119, D4 120, B flat3 121, A3 122) in the solo's register.
% c085 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 54: allow time for arco -> pizz.): 114 violas a2. r4 (were a1 into the pizz. on 115 beat 2), as the cellos and basses.
% c183 (form; A&S Unit 14, PDF 219-220 sec. 10: after a long V key area the tonicized V reverts to its function by taking its minor 7th, 4 of the main key, 'the one scale degree in the tonic key
% that does not appear in the key of the dominant'; Ex. 14-1 bar 11: Schubert puts that 7th in the bass of a V4/2 right after the cadence in the dominant): 133-134 cellos and basses F (was G held under the
% violas' inner F4), so the G7 is a V4/2 and G is a tonic only on 132.1; violas D4 (was F4: the 7th is not doubled, and with Vln II B4 and Vln I G5 the V4/2 is complete at 134 after Hn 3-4 leave). The cadence in G at 131-132 and the G area are kept. The bass F passes to D at 135 inside the
% dominant (voice exchange with the violas' D4-F4; G7 -> B-D-F-A flat); the 7th F stays in Vln II, violas, Cl 2 and Hn 3 at 135 and falls F-E-E flat-D in Vln II to the stop chord at 138.
devVla = { f'2->(\f c'4. des'8 | es'4-.) des'8( c'8 bes4-.) aes4-. | <aes c'>4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | bes'2->(\f f'4. ges'8 | aes'4-.) ges'8( f'8 es'4-.) des'4-. | <des' f'>4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | c'2\p\< c'2 | c'2 bes2 | aes2 aes4 g4 | g4\ff c'8( bes aes g f g) | f8( g aes bes aes2) | g2 c'8( bes aes g) | aes2-> r2 | a2.\p\> r4\! | r4 <g' bes'>4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz. div." r4 <g' bes'>4 | r4 <a' c''>4 r4 <g' bes'>4 | r2 r4 <g' bes'>4 | r4 <g' c''>4 r4 <g' bes'>4 | r4 <a' c''>4 r4 <a' c''>4 | r4 <a' d''>4 r4 <a' d''>4 | r4 <g' bes'>4 r4 g'4 | r4 <a' d''>4 r2 | r1 | r2. \once \override TextScript.avoid-slur = #'outside \once \override TextScript.outside-staff-priority = #600 d'4(\p^\markup \italic "arco, unis." | a'2 g'4 fis'4 | e'2 d'2) | e'1\pp | c'1 | b1\< | d'2->(\mf\< a4. b8 | c'4-.\f) b8( a8 g4-.) fis4-. | <d' b'>4-> r4 r2 | d'1\>~ | d'2. r4\! | }
% c211 (melody; Adler ch. 3, adler1 PDF 99: the cello's D string 'is the most musically captivating string... warm and lyrical', the A string 'the most brilliant and piercing'; PDF 73-74: an open string stands out among stopped notes in an expressive line; PDF 73: timbre is the reason to name a string): 123-124 the cellos' F2 entry D4-B3-A3-G3-B3-D4, p espress., is marked 'sul D' (dashed line to its last note), so the hand-over's first statement is on the warm D string with no open A3 on 124's downbeat; notes, dynamics and the hand-over order unchanged.
devVc = { f2->(\f c4. des8 | es4-.) des8( c8 bes,4-.) aes,4-. | <f c'>4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | bes2->(\f f4. ges8 | aes4-.) ges8( f8 es4-.) des4-. | <bes f'>4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | f2\p\< e2 | es2 d2 | des2 c2 | c'4\ff c'8( bes aes g f g) | f2 des2 | bes,2 c8( bes, aes, g,) | f,2-> r2 | d2.\p\>\upbow r4\! | g4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | fis4 r4 g4 r4 | es4 r4 r2 | c4 r4 bes4 r4 | a4 r4 r2 | d4 r4 r2 | g4 r4 es4 r4 | d4 r4 r2 | \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul D" d'2.(\p^\markup \italic "arco, espress."\startTextSpan b4 | a4 g4 b4 d'4)\stopTextSpan | e1\pp | c2 b,2 | a,1 | d1 | g1\< | d1 | d2\f d2 | g,4-> r4 r2 | f1\>~ | f2. r4\! | }
devCb = { f,2->(\f c,4. des,8 | es,4-.) des,8( c,8 bes,,4-.) aes,,4-. | f,4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | bes,2->(\f f,4. ges,8 | aes,4-.) ges,8( f,8 es,4-.) des,4-. | bes,,4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | f,2\p\< e,2 | es,2 d,2 | des,2 c,2 | c,1\ff | f,2 des,2 | bes,,2 c,2 | f,,2-> r2 | d,2.\p\>\upbow r4\! | g,4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | fis,4 r4 g,4 r4 | es,4 r4 r2 | c,4 r4 bes,4 r4 | a,4 r4 r2 | d,4 r4 r2 | g,4 r4 es,4 r4 | d,4 r4 r2 | g,1\pp^\markup \italic "arco" | fis,2 g,2 | e,1 | c,2 b,,2 | a,,1 | d,1 | g,1\< | d,1 | d,2\f d,2 | g,,4-> r4 r2 | f,1\>~ | f,2. r4\! | }
% c053 (form; A&S Unit 18, PDF 291-292: in a series of 6/3 chords only the beginning and the end are points of articulation, and the end matters for its cadential
% function; Ex. 18-8a, 18-12, PDF 295, 298-299: the 6/3 descent with 7-6 suspensions leads to the cadence): the lament's 6/3 descent (107-109) arrives on V at 110 and the
% F-minor cadence 110-113 is the development's goal. It was f with no trumpets or tuba, so the development's loudest point was below the exposition (27, 91 ff) and the
% retransition (147 ff). Dvorak 9/iv's development peaks ff with the trumpets (177-191) before the recapitulation. 110-113 now ff, trumpets 1-3 and tuba join
% (Tpt 1 on Ob 1's E5-F5 then Fl 1's F5-E5-F5, Tpt 2 = Tbn 1 an octave up and Vln II, Tpt 3 = Hn 1, tuba = basses), timpani C and A flat at 111-112.
% c097 (texture; Adler ch. 11, PDF 44-47, Bartok Concerto for Orchestra i 313-386: the fugato passes from strings to the brass, 'f, ben marc.'; two horns in unison = one trombone): 103-104 Hn 3-4 (a2, with the violas) and Tbn 1 (with the cellos) take the B flat minor head instead of a single punctuation chord, so the second statement is louder and brighter than the first (99, strings alone).
% c098 (expression; A&S Unit 21, PDF 348 sec. 4: dissonant suspensions 'receive something of an accent and their resolutions are softer'; PDF 350 sec. 8: the cadential 4-3 over V is the
% suspension's most important use; Dvorak 9/iv 176, 182, 190: trumpets and trombones ff with the strings at the development's climaxes): 110-113 horns, trumpets, trombones and tuba ff (were f since c054),
% so the climax's 9-10 (111) and cadential 4-3 (112, Tpt 1 and Hn 2 F-E with the violins) are no longer a level below the exposition codetta (91) and the retransition (147), both ff in full brass.
% Answer to c054: the melody's top octave (Vln I, Fl 1, G6-A flat6-F6-E6-F6) lies more than an octave above the highest brass note (Tpt 1 F5), and Tpt 1 itself doubles the G5 octave's F5-E5-F5. Timpani stay f (c056).
% c098: 105 Hn 3-4 and Tbn 1, which double the B flat minor head since c097, end it with the strings' sf. 108 Vln I's restruck 7th C6 over D (the 7-6) takes a tenuto (PDF 347: restruck suspension; PDF 348 sec. 4).
devTptOne = { R1*11 | e''1\ff | f''1 | f''2~( f''4 e''4) | f''2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
devTptTwo = { R1*11 | c''1\ff | c''2 des''2~ | des''2( c''2) | c''2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
% c060 (cyclic lens; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 11: a colour saved for its entrance keeps its surprise; arrival.ily: 223 is the first sustained forte C-major chord in full brass):
% 110 and 112 (second half) Tpt 3 holds B flat 4 (was G4 = Hn 1), so the brass sound V7 of F, not a root-position C-major triad, and the 7th falls to A flat 4 at 111 and 113.
devTptThree = { R1*11 | bes'1\ff | aes'2 aes'2 | bes'1 | aes'2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
devTuba = { R1*11 | c,1\ff | f,,2 des,2 | bes,,2 c,2 | f,,2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
